{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Christopher Jon Weinke (born July 31, 1972) is an American football coach and former player.","title":"Chris Weinke"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Weinke is a former professional American football and baseball player.","title":"Chris Weinke"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" After spending six years in the Toronto Blue Jays minor league baseball system, he enrolled at Florida State University at the age of 26, and played quarterback for the Florida State Seminoles.","title":"Chris Weinke"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He thereafter played professionally in the NFL, where he spent most of his career with the Carolina Panthers.","title":"Chris Weinke"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Christian Matthew "Chris" Michalak (born January 4, 1971) is a former Major League Baseball left-handed pitcher.","title":"Chris Michalak"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" When Michalak played for the Toronto Blue Jays in 2001, he was popular with fans for his relatively advanced age at which he made his Major League debut, appearing as a sort of underdog, as well as having the best move in all of baseball for throwing out a potential base-stealer at first base.","title":"Chris Michalak"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Michalak played for the Washington Nationals Triple-A affiliate, the Columbus Clippers, in .","title":"Chris Michalak"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" On June 14, 2008, Michalak signed a minor league contract with the Florida Marlins and was assigned to their Triple-A affiliate, the Albuquerque Isotopes.","title":"Chris Michalak"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" After his release from the Marlins organization, he signed with the Oakland Athletics and assigned to their Double-A affiliate, the Midland RockHounds, on August 2, 2008.","title":"Chris Michalak"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He became a free agent at the end of the season.","title":"Chris Michalak"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" On April 26, 2009, Michalak signed a minor league deal with the Toronto Blue Jays.","title":"Chris Michalak"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Thomas F. Cheek (June 13, 1939 – October 9, 2005) was an American sportscaster who is best remembered today as the original "Voice of the Toronto Blue Jays", Cheek announced Major League Baseball (MLB) games for the Toronto Blue Jays on radio, as the play-by-play announcer, from the team's establishment in 1977 until his retirement in 2004, in which he had a 27-year streak of 4,306 consecutive games plus 41 post-season games called, which lasted from the first ever Blue Jays game on April 7, 1977 to June 3, 2004.","title":"Tom Cheek"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Cheek was inducted to the Blue Jays Level of Excellence in 2004.","title":"Tom Cheek"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"John Lowell McLaren (born September 29, 1951) is a former Major League Baseball coach and manager, best known for his brief tenure as manager of the Seattle Mariners, from July 1, to June 19, .","title":"John McLaren (baseball)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" A native of the Houston, Texas area, McLaren was a catcher in the Houston Astros minor league system from 1970–1976, and later managed in the Toronto Blue Jays minor league system.","title":"John McLaren (baseball)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He became a major league coach with the Blue Jays in 1986 and has since held major league coaching positions with the Mariners, Boston Red Sox, Cincinnati Reds, Tampa Bay Devil Rays, and Washington Nationals.","title":"John McLaren (baseball)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He also served as a coach for the United States national baseball team during the 2006 World Baseball Classic, spent two nonconsecutive seasons as a scout in the Devil Rays/Rays organization, and was interim manager of the Nationals for three games in 2011.","title":"John McLaren (baseball)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" He was employed as a professional scout for the Oakland Athletics organization from 2012–2015.","title":"John McLaren (baseball)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He will be the catching coach for the Philadelphia Phillies under manager Pete Mackanin for the 2016 season.","title":"John McLaren (baseball)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Toronto Blue Jays farm system consists of eight Minor League Baseball affiliates across the United States and in Canada and the Dominican Republic.","title":"List of Toronto Blue Jays minor league affiliates"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Five teams are independently owned, while three—the Dunedin Blue Jays, Gulf Coast League Blue Jays and Dominican Summer League Blue Jays—are owned by the major league club.","title":"List of Toronto Blue Jays minor league affiliates"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Florence Blue Jays were a minor league baseball team based in Florence, South Carolina.","title":"Florence Blue Jays"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" They began play in the South Atlantic League in 1981 where they captured the league title in 1985.","title":"Florence Blue Jays"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" After the 1986 season the team relocated and became the Myrtle Beach Blue Jays (now the Hagerstown Suns).","title":"Florence Blue Jays"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" They were a minor league club of the Toronto Blue Jays and played at American Legion Stadium.","title":"Florence Blue Jays"}],"peer_messages":["Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Christopher Jon Weinke (born July 31, 1972) is an American football coach and former player.","title":"Chris Weinke"}
